I don't think you can separate content and flavour that easily.

You can see that when hallucinating.  All that changes are the messages 
about your environment, not the "content" in your sense.  Still, I would 
say the game would become unplayable, if you had to hallucinate all the 
time.  You would not be able to avoid attacking your pet, nor to assess 
the difficulty of monsters.  Immediately, the content would have changed 
*with* the flavour...

Moreover, one should clearly distinguish two different lines of 
reasoning from my part.  One in favor of my YANI, in which case I would 
advocate of changing strictly nothing in the messages.  The different 
messages were only a suggestion for the case where this YANI would be 
rejected, in order to achieve a slightly less silly justification for 
the PC's inability to perform different tasks such as teleporting, going 
up a level or going down a level.

In this alternative line of reasoning, which presupposes that the potion 
or amulet is *not* introduced, I was not only suggesting a change of 
messages.  I was rather taking up an idea of yours to make certain 
non-teleport levels accesible to teleportation after killing of their 
"nemesis".  My proposal was that this need not be the case for levels 
such as the castle or Sokoban, because the supposed mechanism for the 
suppression of teleportation was different.  As a consequence, once you 
have killed a demon lord, you will not get the message about the "evil 
mind" preventing you from teleporting anymore and you can teleport, 
whereas on the castle the "invisible walls" will still rise to prevent 
you from teleporting after you have cleaned it out, since they are 
"mechanically"  implemented.  Similarly, I would not remove restrictions 
on teleportation after a rogue has killed the Master Assassin, because 
it is hardly conceivable that the latter keeps the barriers against 
teleportation up by his mind.  It is a device that he has put in use and 
that will continue to exist after his death.

Thus, I was suggesting these different messages with a content-change in 
mind, too.
